  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAn embedded shortwave containing increased moisture currently\nresides around the Tennessee River Valley has trended more\nfavorable to lift northward in conjunction with trough deepening\nout west and ridge building east Monday. The northward lift of\nthis wave acts as a weak warm front with a few more models\nsuggesting enough ascent to squeeze out pockets of light rain\nand/or sprinkles through the day Monday; chances are mainly\nconfined west of I-77 in Ohio in the morning before shifting\ntowards the I-80 corridor through the evening.\n\nThis higher probability of occurrence trend is playing out more\nconfidently in available hi-res guidance versus more pessimistic\nglobal models, resulting in a forecast that could undersell rain\nchances (and oversell temperature) Monday if things like RRFS\nplay out. The mean forecast seems like a fair compromise between\npriors and these recent hi-res trends given dry sub-700mb air in\nthe region may limit any rain from being \"accumulating\".\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nWarm southwest flow with height rises Monday into through\nmidweek support dry weather with temperature rising nearly 20 \ndegrees above average by Wednesday afternoon (though record\nhigh temperatures are unlikely).\n\nEnsemble trends suggest the advertised surface low/trough combo\nbringing widespread rain chances late Wednesday into Thursday \ntracks NW of the region, which could result in a less concrete\nswath of rain as jet ascent potentially fades as it arrives. It\nwould also, though, offer limited (but non-zero) chances for\nenough buoyancy to allow for a lightning strike or two; severe\nchances remains very low. After the initial wave passes, dry\nslotting may usher a break in rain chances with marginal post-\nfrontal cold advection merely dropping temperature to only 5-10\ndegrees above normal. Variability in synoptic patterns among\nensembles members thereafter that suggests little confidence\nbeyond a potentially more active pattern with near to slightly\nabove normal temperature and minimal impacts.",
